Z||000000||Spyro86SE||05-21-2001||05:17 PM||||i just started to sand my car down and i was using 600 grit paper i'm not sure if that is the right grit or not so if any one knows a better grit to use please let me know and also if any one primed there own car what kind of primer did you use i saw this stuff at pep boys it was made for flexiable bumpers it said to spray it on first then use the paint or primer if any one knows please let me know <P>thanks in advance <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/spryologo_1_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroLisa||05-21-2001||05:32 PM||||I didn't spray the paint on my car, but I did all the prep work. Scuffed up paint takes the place of primer. I don't know the name of them, but they are just scuffing pads you can buy probably at a lot of places, but I got mine at an auto paint place. The only places primed on my car were where stone chips made it necessary to use a sander and get under the paint, and where we made some repairs with fiberglass. Pics of the fun couple weeks of sanding: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/carpaint.html" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierolisa/carpaint.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||05-21-2001||06:58 PM||||Hey Spyro!<BR>Get some scotch brite pads from any auto paint store, if you are doing it by hand...<BR>Best results are when you remove and paint a panel, rather than do it on the car...<BR>They also have books there for DIY...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-21-2001||07:19 PM||||I'm more in favor of priming. Especially if you're changing colors. When I did mine, I used a D/A sander with 400, then I wet sanded with 600. I primed with a heavy build primer. At the very least, you should prime anyplace you go through the existing paint to the body, and then wet sand with 600 or 1000 to smooth out the edges. Any bodywork or repairs also should be primed. I removed all the body panels, and sanded, primed, and painted. (I was going from black to white, so it was important to me to get behind all the trim and seams) Then I remounted all the panels, and will shoot another color coat over the whole thing to balance the color among all the panels. Finally finishing with clear. The product you're referring to for the fascias is "Bulldog" it's an adhesion promoter. Use it for any flexible parts, including trim you plan to paint. It will help, it works great. My general philosophy is with paint, do it right or don't do it. And prep is 90% of the paint quality.<BR>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||05-21-2001||10:56 PM||||Hey spyro! (?)<p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||pontiacs4me||05-21-2001||11:51 PM||||All right, first off, sanded paint is called substrate.  You can spray your new paint over sanded paint as long as its all sanded (no shiny areas whatsoever, or it wont stick)  Depending on what color you are going with, will decide on what grit of sandpaper you need to prep it with.  Example, white 320-400, black 600.  In between colors 400.  Real dark in between colors 600.  Now, you can spray over the substrate, but I recommend using a sealing primer first.  That sprays on in one coat, just before the color coat.  No sanding required.  <BR>If you are doing repairs, use a urethane based catalyzed primer surfacer.  with a flex additive.  Primer drys real hard and brittle without it.  If the panel has to flex, but the primer doesnt want to, it will crack, and possibly chip off.  <BR>Now you dont have to remove all the panels for the best results,  Just be sure if you are doing a color change, to tape off all the jambs, and take your time.  If you really desire painting your panels off the car, then reassembling it, good luck with your color match.  There are inconsistencies within the color of most any paint.  Expecially metallic colors.  If you want to prep the panels off the car, go right ahead, but please paint the car complete,  (minus mirrors, spoiler, and headlight doors, if you like).  Unless you want to find out how many different shades of one color there really is.  <P>One more thing, use wet/dry sandpaper when prepping your panels, use it wet of course. if you decide to prep the entire car with scotchbrite pads, I hope you have about 2 weeks off from work, and about 30 bucks for pads.  You can sand an entire fiero down to useable substrate with about 5 sheets of 400g paper.  And in about 3 hours.  At least I can.<p>[This message has been edited by pontiacs4me (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||LZeitgeist||05-22-2001||08:53 AM||||Actually, a 'substrate' is whatever surface you are applying your work to... it's not just another name for 'sanded paint'. <P>If you make a sign with a paintbrush on a piece of bare plywood, the plywood is the substrate.<P>If you do a charcoal sketch on a piece of artist's paper, the artist's paper is the substrate.<P>If you apply vinyl graphics to a car door, the painted metal/plastic of the door panel is the substrate.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||pontiacs4me||05-22-2001||11:29 AM||||That is what I was implying, it was late, and I was getting very dislexic typing, and I couldnt think straight.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||05-22-2001||11:56 AM||||Pontiac, Ive been in body & paint business since mid 60's. I dont believe in flex ever, but Ill beg to differ about using it in primer. It specificly says 'DO NOT USE IN PRIMER/ SURFACERS'right on the can. But thats every painters choice how they prefer theres done. The finer the paper, the better easier to wet sand after. I do everycar with scotchbrites where possible, 400 on everything else. Your gonna sand the finished job to buff it out anyway, so why work yourself to death with 600 or finer. As for scotchbrite, I seldom need more than one for an all over paint job, cost .50. scotchbrite is far easier to get in tite around parts and seams too. Late model cars I scuff wet with soapy water, then rinse and blow dry.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||pontiacs4me||05-23-2001||02:16 PM||||The flex additive I have doesnt say anything about primer,  it says urethane flex additive.  You can use it in anything urethane based.  I have a 73 firebird with the endura rubber front bumper, and I had a problem with the primer cracking off the bumper before.  So last year when I painted my bird, again, I used flex additive in my primer for the bumper, and flex add, in my clear.  It still looks great.  The first time i painted my bird, about 3 years ago, the primer cracked under the paint, after about 5 months.  It just cured out until it was so hard, and so brittle, that something moved it, and it cracked.  I painted my car in may of 2000, and it is still flexable.  <BR>To each their own.||||reg||1
Z||000010||AusFiero||05-23-2001||08:46 PM||||Well I might as well throw my Aussie body shop experinece in here.<BR>600 grade is best for rubbing down any car for paint, wet of course.<BR>Primer only the areas that need doing if you are keeping the same colour, even though a full prime is always better.<BR>If you want a real deep paint job and are using acrylic paints follow this method.<P>Make sure primer is baby bum smooth with no imperfections what so ever. We get the perfections out before the paint down here...not buff them out later.<P>Next spray 4 coats of colour and let it cure for a week. Block it down with 600 grade paper to be without imperfections again. Apply another 3 coats of colour.<P>Let this cure a week and then block it down with 1200 grade paper. Mask it up again ready for the clear coats.<P>Spray 2 coats of 50% Clear and 50% Colour. The spray 2 coats of 25% colour and 75% clear. Next finish it off with 4 coats of clear. For real show quality make it 4 coats of each with more rubbing back inbetween.<P>Block it down again with 1200 grade wet and dry paper then your car should be smooth enough to buff down by hand. Show me a car without  1 swirlmark and I know you will be showing me a hand finished car.<P>The paint depth and shine you get from that method is unbelievable. You can peer into the paint and be happy with how much depth it has. Shine on paint is easy to get. A true show quality finish has depth.<P>Some will disagree with these methods but thats life. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero.gif"> <BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000011||pontiacs4me||05-24-2001||02:10 AM||||seems like a bunch of sanding, and clearcoating, but I guess it must look nice in the end.  I just spray the basecoat, whatever it takes to cover the substrate, 3 to 4 coats, then about 4 coats of clear.  Sometimes I will use 1500 grit wet to sand, then buff with 3m compound, then with 3m foam polishing pad glaze on a black foam pad for the final finish.  sometimes I will sand the clear with 600 and give the car one more coat of clear (flow coat), depends on how much the customer wants to spend.  I just sprayed 5 coats of clear on my firebird, and sanded and buffed with the above mentioned steps, and it is so deep you would think it was another dimension, that you could just walk into.  And of course the 200 hours of bodywork helps too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Rob Durham||05-24-2001||10:25 AM||||The most important thing to do is get the body as perfect as possible, if you don't you will see the flaws under the paint. <P>The best way to do that is block the entire car with 180 grit then use a good high build primer spray 2-3 coats then block the entire car down with 320 grit, spray it again, block again with 600 grit. The show quality paint jobs are done like this only we prime and block the car out 7-10 times and then I use a sealer to prevent any bleed through before we spray the base coat (4-5 coats) then 5 coats of clear because you will block at least 2 coats back off to get the finish like glass.<P>We charge $4500.00 to do this Not counting any actual body work that may be needed. Yes its a lot of work BUT quality takes time and patience!<P>Rob<p>[This message has been edited by Rob Durham (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||AusFiero||05-24-2001||11:04 AM||||Actually on taking Fiero panels back to the base materials has anyone ever tried bead blasting, or what have people found effective ? Was just wondering because my car has already been resprayed and the next time...which it needs I would rather start from scratch and don't really feel like rubbing it all back to get the paint off. Don't really trust paint strippers with the panels makeup. Anyone used them with any problems?<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero.gif"> <BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
